div.p:
Twenty-six patients with LET were assessed for eligibility, and six of them were excluded (two declined to participate and four did not meet the inclusion criteria) (Fig. 2
                     ). Finally, 20 subjects (12 men and 8 women) were included and completed all the experimental procedures. No adverse events were reported. The mean age (± standard deviation [SD]) of the participants was (49.6 ​± ​6.6) years, with a mean body mass index (±SD) of (25.8 ​± ​3.1) kg·m−2. The median (IQR) duration of symptoms was 10 (6–24) weeks (Table 1
                     ). The homogeneity of variance indicated non-significant differences in all outcomes except for the PPT in the affected lateral epicondyle. Accordingly, we utilized a compound symmetry variance-covariance structure for most outcomes and an unstructured covariance structure for the PPT of the affected lateral epicondyle. Also, we transformed the dependent variable to stabilize variance and applied Restricted Maximum Likelihood estimation to accurately estimate model parameters. The results of the homogeneity of variance, as well as the unadjusted and adjusted main effects, interactions, and significant effects of covariates, are presented in the Supplementary Table 1.

A statistically significant within-group change was found only in the BFR group between pre- and post-exercise at the extensor carpi radialis brevis (−1.6, 95% CI: −2.6 to −0.5; p ​= ​0.011). The within-group comparisons at the remaining measurement sites resulted in non-significant differences for both intervention groups (
                        Supplementary Table 2
                        ).
